{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Evidence relocated from the description (verbatim below), so the description reads as a PLAN.\nNothing is deleted. This was forced by a real constraint: widening `file_impact` to match the\nmerged commit required a fresh plan-review, and the 11,782-char description exceeded the\n8,000-char admission cap -- the composition trap filed as `exorcismal-tokophobic-agama` (4bd0).\n\n## The measured finding: none of the eleven is a sparse counter\n\nThe inline Terraform comments justify `notBreaching` with a belief about the publisher — e.g. 'No X errors published in a period is the healthy steady state'. **The publisher contradicts that comment.**\n\n`infra/scripts/observability.sh` implements every host counter as an OFFSET-DELTA, not an event emit: it greps a cumulative journal, subtracts a persisted offset, and UNCONDITIONALLY publishes the delta (`--value \"$new\"`), which is `0` when nothing happened. `publish_autodeploy_marker_delta` (~line 296) is the shared helper for six of them. The probe is a systemd timer at `OnUnitActiveSec=5min` (infra/scripts/install-observability.sh:31).\n\nSo a healthy period publishes `0` — it does NOT publish nothing. Missing data therefore means THE PUBLISHER IS DEAD, exactly as with the disk alarms.\n\nLive CloudWatch confirms it. Sampling 2026-09-01 12:00-14:00Z at 5-min periods returned dense datapoints, all `0.0`, for every one of the eleven.\n\n## AC2 evidence — replayed, not waited for (2026-09-03)\n\nReplayed 24 hours of each alarm's OWN recorded series against its LIVE configuration, modelling CloudWatch's evaluation exactly (period grid with gaps materialised, `treat_missing_data` applied to missing periods, `datapoints_to_alarm` of `evaluation_periods` for both the ALARM and OK transitions):\n\n    alarm                                                  per/ep/dta  pts  miss  transitions\n    rebar-autodeploy-errors                                  900/4/2    96     0       2\n    rebar-autodeploy-mcp-mem-abort                           900/4/2    96     0       0\n    rebar-autodeploy-mcp-retire-cap                          900/4/2    96     0       0\n    rebar-autodeploy-review-interrupts-bound-exceeded        900/4/2    96     0       2\n    rebar-autodeploy-review-interrupts-signal-unavailable    900/4/2    96     0       0\n    rebar-gerrit-data-disk-high                              300/3/2   261    27       0\n    rebar-gerrit-g2p-dispatch-errors                         300/3/2   261    27       0\n    rebar-gerrit-mirror-out-of-sync                          300/3/2   261    27       2\n    rebar-gerrit-replication-errors                          300/3/2   261    27       0\n    rebar-gerrit-voter-errors                                300/5/3   261    27       0\n    rebar-review-bot-merge-change-errors                     300/3/2   261    27       0\n    rebar-root-disk-pressure                                 300/3/2   261    27       0\n\n**The jitter is real and was absorbed.** Every 300s alarm saw **27 missing periods** out of ~288 — the ~22-of-24 pattern measured before the change, now confirmed over a full day. Under the OLD 1-of-1 configuration each of those 27 gaps would have been an immediate page once `treat_missing_data` became breaching. Under the widened 3-of-2 windows, eight of them produced **zero** transitions.\n\n**The three alarms that DID transition are TRUE POSITIVES, not flaps.** Checked each against its raw series for nonzero readings:\n\n    mirror_out_of_sync                 1.0 at 09-02 17:19, 09-02 19:34, 09-03 15:29\n    review_interrupts_bound_exceeded   1.0 at 09-02 16:09, 09-02 16:54\n    deploy_errors                      2.0-4.0 across 09-02 15:39-16:29 (the febd uv-pin build failures)\n\nSo every transition in 24 hours was driven by a genuine breaching VALUE. **Zero transitions anywhere were caused by missing data.** That is precisely what the widened windows were for.\n\n## Why this replaces the original 24-hour-observation wording\n\nThe operator's point, and it is correct: an acceptance criterion that depends on time passing is defective. Waiting samples whichever day happens to elapse, proves nothing about a worse one, cannot be re-run, and — as this replay shows — would not even have distinguished a flap from a true positive without exactly this analysis anyway. The replay is deterministic, repeatable, took minutes, and covers a window deliberately chosen to include the worst churn on record (a 5-hour Gerrit outage, a 12-hour MCP outage, five failed deploys and three MCP cutovers).\n\nWorth flagging separately: `mirror_out_of_sync` fired again at **09-03 15:29 today**, a genuine mirror divergence — the alarm is doing real work, not sitting decorative."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788575626271478001,"uuid":"f3231e83-ded3-49e5-9bdd-789573f72e4e"}